  "brief": {
    "problem": "Managing database workload within strict Neon Free plan limits (100 compute-unit hours per month) while maintaining system responsiveness and avoiding unnecessary resource use.",
    "decision": "Configured the production endpoint with fixed minimum and maximum compute of 0.25 compute units and a five-minute autosuspend delay. Updated handling of signed integration requests to validate timing and return early without database access when outside configured intake and publishing windows. Changed source intake to use hourly windows and publication handling to use exact configured windows. Modified background dashboard polling to pause when the page was hidden, unfocused, or after 60 seconds without activity, resuming only when active and recently used.",
    "evidence": "The repository anit0607/ai-journey-studio (commit 0453b6c7d36a76b3bd306c02dc0c9c854db452d1, dated 25 October 2026) documented system observations showing production endpoint was suspended at 04:10:55, 04:12:20, and 04:14:50 UTC, indicating autosuspend was effective without manual intervention or triggering SQL queries during that period. A separate Upstash metadata observation confirmed the scheduled 04:12 integration wake succeeded without SQL trace or response body. The verification used an existing Neon Free account without paid upgrade. The notes clarify that observations confirm endpoint state only at sampled times, not continuous suspension duration nor direct monthly compute savings measurement.",
    "useful_detail": "This configuration and scheduling method offers a concrete approach for maintaining endpoint suspension during inactivity under strict free-tier constraints by aligning request handling with defined time windows and dynamically controlling dashboard polling activity, observable remotely via provider status without manual intervention."
  }
}
